# Session unlock and auto-fill flows

## Session unlock (legacy vault controller — `popup/popup.ts` mounted inside the sidebar)

The side panel has three modes: `setup`, `locked`, and `unlocked`. These are owned by the legacy vault controller (`src/popup/popup.ts`, named "popup" for historical reasons — there is no popup surface). The sidebar entrypoint (`src/sidebar/sidebar.ts`) dynamic-imports that controller unless `ONBOARDING_V2_ENABLED` is true (or `#onboarding=1` is present in dev/e2e builds), in which case it mounts `onboarding/render.ts` instead.

On every side panel open, `init()` checks `browser.storage.session` for a saved master password (`SESSION_MASTER_KEY = "cunySessionMaster"`). If found, it runs PBKDF2 + AES-GCM decrypt automatically and opens directly in `unlocked` mode. If decryption fails (e.g. vault was re-keyed), the session entry is purged and the side panel falls back to `locked`.

The master password is written to `browser.storage.session` after every successful unlock or save. It is cleared immediately when the user clicks **Lock vault**, which also resets all in-memory session state.

In **unlocked** mode, users can optionally set a new master password (both "new" fields must match); saving re-encrypts the vault.

`browser.storage.session` is not available in Firefox < 115 or Chrome < 102. The three session helpers (`saveSessionMaster`, `loadSessionMaster`, `clearSessionMaster`) wrap the API in try/catch and silently degrade to always-locked behaviour on unsupported browsers.

## Auto-fill flow

1. **On each ssologin.cuny.edu tab load** (`content.ts`): `autoFill()` sends `{ type: "AUTO_FILL_REQUEST" }` to the service worker.
2. **Service worker** (`service-worker.ts`): Reads session master + encrypted vault from storage, decrypts, returns `{ success: true, payload }` or a failure reason (`no_session_master`, `no_vault`, `decrypt_error`). **Onboarding fallback:** when no vault is set up yet and the sidebar has staged onboarding credentials via `STAGE_ONBOARDING_CREDENTIALS`, the SW returns those (with `totpSecret: ""`) so Screen 4 can log the student in. The staging buffer is module-scope memory only — see the security rule. If the user never unlocked this session and onboarding isn't running, auto-fill does nothing.
3. **Content script** (`main()`): Chooses behaviour by URL using helpers from `src/cuny/ssoSite.ts` (`matchesCredentialPage`, `matchesTotpPage`). `fillCredentials` / `fillTotp` wait for Oracle JET–rendered controls, set values in a Knockout-aware way, then click submit / Verify.

Oracle JET renders inputs after `document_idle`, so the content script uses a `MutationObserver` + timeouts instead of assuming immediate DOM.

4. **Manual test** (`debugPanel.ts`, `npm run build:dev` only): **Send test FILL_CREDENTIALS to active tab** sends the in-memory `sessionPayload` to the active tab. Useful when the page was already open before unlock or for debugging. The `browser.runtime.onMessage` listener in `content.ts` that handles `FILL_CREDENTIALS` is gated with `if (import.meta.env.DEV)` — it is **absent from the production IIFE bundle**. Vite statically evaluates the guard and tree-shakes the listener in production builds.

## TOTP enroll secret scraping flow

When the user navigates to the Oracle RUI "add authentication method" page to enroll a new TOTP factor, the extension captures the displayed Base32 secret automatically so the user does not have to copy-paste it into the side panel.

1. **Content script** (`watchTotpSecretOnEnrollPage`): On pages matching `matchesTotpEnrollPage`, waits via `MutationObserver` for the element identified by `TOTP_SECRET_DISPLAY_ARIA_LABELLEDBY`. Normalizes the candidate string (strips whitespace, uppercases, strips trailing `=`; rejects if not valid Base32 length/alphabet) and sends `{ type: "TOTP_SECRET_FROM_PAGE", secret }` to the service worker.
2. **Service worker**: Validates the secret again (same normalization) and writes it to `browser.storage.session` under `PENDING_TOTP_SECRET_SESSION_KEY`.
3. **Side panel** (`storage.session.onChanged` listener): Detects the new key, reads it, pre-fills the TOTP secret input, and clears the session key. The user sees the field pre-populated and just saves.

The scraped secret is sensitive plaintext — see the **Security, crypto, and gotchas** rule.

## MFA self-service verify OTP flow

After the user enrolls a new TOTP factor via the Oracle self-service portal, the IdP requires a one-time verification code before activating the factor. The relevant page is `…/oaa/rui/index.html?h_ra=1`.

1. **Content script** (`startMfaEnrollVerifyOtpPolling`): On pages matching `matchesRuiMfaEnrollVerifyPage`, starts a `setInterval` at `RUI_MFA_ENROLL_VERIFY_POLL_INTERVAL_MS`. On each tick, looks for the input identified by `RUI_MFA_ENROLL_VERIFY_OTP_INPUT_ID`.
2. When the field appears (the user clicked **Verify Now**), the script sends `AUTO_FILL_REQUEST` to the service worker, generates a fresh TOTP code from the vault secret, and fills the field. The interval clears itself after a successful fill.

Polling is used here instead of `MutationObserver` because the Oracle SPA re-renders the form in ways that made observers flaky — see the comment in `startMfaEnrollVerifyOtpPolling`.

## Onboarding message bridge

When `mountOnboarding` is active (sidebar), `onboarding/render.ts` registers a `runtime.onMessage` listener that routes known `ONBOARDING_*` messages through `applyOnboardingMessage(controller, message)`:

- `ONBOARDING_CREDENTIAL_ERROR { culprit }` → stashes the culprit on the controller and routes the sidebar to `EMAIL_ENTRY` (culprit === "email") or `PASSWORD_ENTRY` (default), with an inline red banner above the affected input. The content script emits this only after seeing the `#serverError` DOM marker on `/auth_cred_submit` — URL alone is insufficient (plan-05, commit `0525eed`).
- `ONBOARDING_STAGE_DETECTED { stage: "allow_gate" }` → advances `OPENING_CUNY` to `ALLOW_GATE`.
- `ONBOARDING_REOPEN_CUNY_TAB` is handled by the service worker: it opens `CUNY_LOGIN_ENTRY_URL` (or the provided `url`) in a new tab. Used by Screen 4–10a when the student closed the CUNY tab mid-flow.
- `ONBOARDING_OVERLAY_COMMAND` / `ONBOARDING_VERIFY_STATUS` / `ONBOARDING_TAB_REATTACHED` are validated and acknowledged by the service worker today; plans 06+ wire their sidebar-side handlers.

On sidebar unmount, `mountOnboarding` fires `CLEAR_ONBOARDING_CREDENTIALS` so the service worker drops its staged credentials.

## Extension banner on the CUNY tab

`src/content/banner.ts` mounts an extension-branded credential-error banner on the CUNY page when the content script detects wrong credentials (`mountCredentialErrorBanner`). Styles are inlined with INT_MAX z-index so CUNY's CSS can't hide it, and the element is attached to `document.documentElement` to survive late JET mutations. Mount/unmount are idempotent.

## SSO site constants

All URL path markers, DOM element IDs, and timing constants are defined in `src/cuny/ssoSite.ts`. Never hardcode paths, input IDs, or TOTP parameters in `content.ts` or `service-worker.ts` — always import from `ssoSite.ts`. This is the single place to update if CUNY changes their pages.

For live page structure — selectors, timing, DOM skeletons, and the full transition graph — consult `.map/`. It is the canonical reference for writing Playwright fixtures and onboarding automation against the real CUNY SSO site without opening a browser. Start with `.map/README.md`, then the relevant `pages/*.md` file and `conventions.md` for cross-cutting rules (Oracle JET async behaviour, `setInputValue` vs keystroke simulation per input, etc.).
